{"type":"user","message":{"content":"\n# Step Coverage Validation\n\n## Purpose\nEvaluate whether one scenario from the scenario registry is executable:\nwhether every one of its steps binds to a step definition in the suite\nthat owns it.\n\n## Instructions\n1. Read the architectural spec listed under Reference Documentation and\n   the scenario's tests live in `tests/\u003cservice\u003e/`, where `service:`\n   equals the subject's **Service**. That entry's `path:` is the suite\n   root; its step definitions live under `\u003cpath\u003e/steps/`.\n2. Glob and Read every file under `\u003csuite path\u003e/steps/` and collect the\n   registered patterns — each is the first argument of a\n   `suite.Step(`\u003cregexp\u003e`, …)` call.\n3. Match each of the subject's steps against those patterns, using the\n   step TEXT only: the Given/When/Then/And keyword is not part of what a\n   definition binds.\n4. Answer the validation question against what you found on disk.\n5. Always explain your reasoning BEFORE the answer block.\n\nDo not assume a definition exists because one plainly should. A pattern\nyou did not read is a pattern that is not there.\n\n## Reference Documentation\n- Read(`./docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`) — architecture_yaml\n\n## Subject — Registry Scenario\n\n**Scenario ID:** INT-900\n**Description:** MCP /mcp endpoint responds 200 to a valid initialize POST\n**Service:** mcp-service\n**Requirement:** fixture seed — expects `build tests` to write the step definitions\n\n### Steps\nGiven:\n  - the MCP server is running on its configured port\nWhen:\n  - the Claude User posts a valid JSON-RPC initialize request to /mcp\nThen:\n  - the server returns HTTP 200\n\n\n---\n\n## Validation Question\n\nDoes every step of this scenario resolve to a step definition\nin the suite that owns it?\n\nFirst find the suite. Read the architectural spec listed under\nReference Documentation and look through\nthe tree `tests/\u003cservice\u003e/`, where `service:`\nequals this scenario's **Service**. That entry's `path:` is\nthe suite's root; its step definitions live under\n`\u003cpath\u003e/steps/`.\n\nThen bind each step. A step definition is a call of the form\n`suite.Step(`\u003cregexp\u003e`, \u003cfunc\u003e)`. A step RESOLVES when exactly\none registered regexp matches its text — the text alone, with\nthe Given/When/Then/And keyword stripped, because a definition\nis bound by wording and not by which block a step sits in.\n\nOne exception, and it is not a loophole: a step whose text\nopens with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a MODEL rather than by\na step definition. It always resolves and it must never be\ngiven one — a definition matching `judge: …` would bind a\nclause written precisely because no pattern could settle it.\nMark such a step MODEL-RUN and move on.\n\nPass when every step of the scenario resolves.\nFail when any step resolves to none, or to more than one.\n\nContext: emit one line per step, then a verdict.\n  - \"step: \u003cstep text\u003e -\u003e \u003cmatching pattern, MODEL-RUN, or UNDEFINED\u003e\"\n  - \"Verdict: executable / \u003cN\u003e step(s) unbound\"\n SO THAT WE ENSURE A scenario no step definition binds is behaviour written down and never run; the registry would claim coverage the suite does not have.\n\n## If Validation Fails\nIf your answer is `fail`, copy the following fix template VERBATIM into\nthe `fix_prompt:` field of your result. Do not paraphrase.\n\nAuthor the missing step definition(s) in the suite that owns\nthis scenario.\n\nSteps:\n  1. Read the architectural spec and find the\n     tree `tests/\u003cservice\u003e/`, where `service:`\n     matches this scenario's. Its `path:` is the suite root.\n  2. Read the existing definitions under `\u003csuite path\u003e/steps/`\n     to learn the state type they share and the helpers\n     already available. REUSE them — a new definition that\n     re-implements what a sibling already does is the drift\n     this command exists to prevent.\n  3. For each unbound step, register one definition:\n     `suite.Step(`^\u003canchored regexp\u003e$`, \u003cfunc\u003e)`. Put a\n     capture group where the step's text varies — a quoted\n     run, a number — so one definition serves every scenario\n     phrasing it that way, and do not write a definition whose\n     pattern is a literal copy of one scenario's line.\n  4. Implement the function against the suite's state type.\n     Given prepares, When acts, Then asserts and returns an\n     error naming what it expected and what it got.\n  5. Do not weaken an existing definition to make this step\n     match. Do not edit the registry.\n  6. Never write a definition for a step whose text opens with\n     `llm:` or `judge:`. Those are run by a model by the\n     scenario author's explicit choice, and a pattern that\n     captured one would take that choice back.\n\n\n## Answer Format\nOutput your answer using this exact format:\n\n=== FILE_START: tmp/{{RUN_DIR}}/01-INT-900-checklist-build-tests-implementation-result.yaml ===\nanswer: \u003cpass | fail\u003e\ncontext:\n  - \"\u003ccontext line per the question's context spec\u003e\"\nfix_prompt: |\n  \u003cif answer is fail, paste the fix template above verbatim\u003e\n  \u003cif answer is pass, omit this field entirely\u003e\n=== FILE_END: tmp/{{RUN_DIR}}/01-INT-900-checklist-build-tests-implementation-result.yaml ===\n\n\n**ANSWER CONTRACT FOR THIS RUN — this supersedes the FILE_START/FILE_END\ninstructions above.** Your final message must be a single JSON object:\n\n- `answer`: exactly `\"pass\"` or `\"fail\"`.\n- `context`: an array of one-line observation strings, as described above.\n- `fix_prompt`: the fix instructions when the answer is `fail`; omit or\n  leave empty otherwise.\n\nDo not emit FILE_START/FILE_END markers, and do not wrap the object in\nmarkdown fences. Reasoning before the object is fine and is ignored.\n\n","role":"user"},"session_id":"default"}
